detail:
A RiotTextFact holds only slot, kind, clause and source, and the chip shows only the kind's word (RIOT_TEXT_KIND_WORDS). Accepted rows from the review that will overstate at a glance:
- Xin Zhao R "Invulnerable" for "immune to damage from enemies outside the sweep range";
- Pantheon E "Invulnerable" for "invulnerable to damage from the front";
- Swain E "Cooldown refund" for "a shorter cooldown during Demonic Ascension" (the review treats the kind as "refund or reduction");
- Soraka W "Heal" under Sustain for "heal another friendly champion";
- Janna E "Shield" for "an ally champion or turret".

The docs note that the two texts disagree on at least six abilities, but there is no field for that. Checked facts carry `appliesTo`; facts from Riot's text do not.

verdict.reason:
The gap is real, but not where the finding puts it. Its five example rows do not show it, and two of its three proposed fixes are wrong or optional. Nothing was edited; the worktree is clean at 9cce9a33.

WHAT HOLDS (reproduced)
- The fact record has nowhere to put a condition, a recipient, an "also described as" or "Riot's texts differ". RiotTextFact (types.ts:503), the importer's facts.push, parseRiotText and riotTextView keep only slot, kind, clause and source. The chip word is RIOT_TEXT_KIND_WORDS[kind].
- The final reviewed table, scratchpad/mi/reviewed, has all four:
  - 68 `qualifier` (4 with `qualifierSource`);
  - 44 `conditional`, which with the qualifiers makes 108 facts, 105 of them on champions without checked notes;
  - 11 `alsoDescribedAs`;
  - 7 `disagreements`, 6 of them on champions the page would show.
- Its "For the app" section requires the page to show:
  - the qualifier, in Riot's words;
  - "Conditional: see Riot's text";
  - "also described as <kind>";
  - "Riot's two texts differ", with both quotes.
- The importer drops them all. Run into scratch (only `review` turned into the object it reads), it wrote 173 files and 1,112 facts, reported 0 left out, and kept 0 markers and 0 disagreements.
- The page could not show them either. I rendered MatchupContent server-side with the branch's own view and components for 10 pairs, with the imported files. Then I merged the markers back into the JSON and rendered again. The HTML was byte-identical; the view's facts still held only kind, clause and source; no page said "conditional" or "texts differ".
- Chips that would overstate, from that render:
  - Xin Zhao Q "Knock-up" (tip: "knocking an opponent into the air."; the review marks it conditional);
  - Xin Zhao P "Heal" (conditional);
  - Gnar W "Stun" (tip: "stunning enemies in an area."; qualifier "Mega Gnar" lost);
  - Morgana E "Shield" (tip: "The shield prevents Disabling and Immobilizing effects until it breaks."; qualifier "an ally champion" lost);
  - Zilean R "Stasis" and "Heal" (qualifier "an allied champion" lost).
- Disputed effects vanish without a word: Zac R shows only "Slow", and Camille E and Janna Q show none of their disputed crowd control.

WHAT DOES NOT HOLD
- **The five cited rows show no gap.** None carries a marker in the review. Each clause states its own condition or recipient, and the rendered tip quotes it:
  - Xin Zhao R: "immune to damage from enemies outside the sweep range";
  - Pantheon E: "from the front";
  - Soraka W: "heal another friendly champion";
  - Janna E: "an ally champion or turret";
  - Swain E: "shorter cooldown during Demonic Ascension".
- That display is by design. Owner decision 4 puts the quoted clause one hover or tap away. The review adds a qualifier only for what the clause leaves out. The proposed fields would change none of these rows. "Invulnerable · conditional" would be a marker the review never gave.
- **appliesTo for these facts should not be added.** The review records no such field. A recipient the clause omits comes as `qualifier`, in Riot's words. Inventing appliesTo would add claims to a layer that is "kinds only". Checked facts also give their subject only in the tip.
- **The rename is an optional nit.** 54 of the 92 cooldownrefund clauses say "reduced". Most mean seconds taken off, which the app's own CooldownRefund already calls a refund. About 7 are a shorter cooldown in a state, which "Cooldown refund" misnames: Swain E, K'Sante Q and E, Kindred Q, Kayn E, Taliyah Q, Nasus R. Their tips quote the clause.

WHAT THE IMPORT NEEDS
- Optional `qualifier`, `qualifierSource`, `conditional` and `alsoDescribedAs` on the fact and its view.
- `disagreements` for each slot on the champion's record and the ability's view.
- The importer copying and checking them, and parseRiotText reading them.
- The chip's tip and the Details window showing each in the review's words; "also described as" never as a second chip; "Riot's two texts differ" on the tile and on the assembled side.

The importer also refuses the table as it is: `review` is a sentence, and the counts are in `reviewRecord`. That is separate from this finding.

Evidence: (coordinator scratch)/mi-review2-regressions/refute-chips/ (tools/render.mts, tools/parse.cjs, html/, out/, import.log). The review's instructions: (coordinator scratch)/mi/reviewed/_summary.md, section "For the app".

detail:
The L2 facts are labelled as checked in several places:
- each chip's tip: "Checked notes · Source: …";
- the Details window heading: "CHECKED FACTS";
- the tiles heading: "Curated notes: checked on patch 26.22";
- the level-note tips: "Checked notes."

The same screen says "(not by a person)" for Riot's-text facts and "No person has checked Riven vs Darius as a pair" for the assembled pair. A reader will take L2 to have been checked by a person. docs/MATCHUP_INTEL.md says the opposite: "No person has checked any of it… the 6 champions' 99 facts were drafted and audited by AI agents". This is visible on every pair with a curated champion, for example Riven vs Darius, all 1,308 chips I swept.
